# {{ N.mcp }}.2) Connect a Coding Agent

On this page, you'll add both Genie MCP servers to a coding agent and ask it the question that tripped up Genie in {{ N.agent }}.2. A coding agent is the fastest way to try an MCP server before you write any code, and it lets your engineers use governed numbers while they build.

This page is optional if you don't use a coding agent. You can go straight to [{{ N.mcp }}.3)](6-3-product-agent.md).

## Task 1 - Install the Unity Gateway CLI

The **Unity Gateway CLI** (`ug`) signs you in to Databricks and writes the MCP configuration for Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and other agents.

1. Install `ug` with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), then check the version:

    ```bash
    uv tool install git+https://github.com/databricks/unity-gateway
    ug --version
    ```

2. Sign in to your workspace and let `ug` set up your agents:

    ```bash
    ug configure --workspace https://<workspace-hostname>
    ```

## Task 2 - Add the two MCP servers

=== "Claude Code"

    1. Add the Genie One MCP:

        ```bash
        ug mcp add --agents claude --names system.ai.genie_one_mcp
        ```

    2. Add your Genie Agent MCP with the `genie-space:` selector and your space ID from {{ N.mcp }}.1:

        ```bash
        ug mcp add --agents claude --names "genie-space:$GENIE_SPACE_ID"
        ```

    3. Start Claude Code through Unity Gateway, then enter `/mcp` and check that both servers are connected:

        ```bash
        ug claude
        ```

=== "Cursor"

    1. Add both servers to Cursor. `ug` writes them to `~/.cursor/mcp.json`, which the Cursor IDE and Cursor Agent both read:

        ```bash
        ug mcp add --agents cursor --names system.ai.genie_one_mcp
        ug mcp add --agents cursor --names "genie-space:$GENIE_SPACE_ID"
        ```

    2. Restart Cursor and check that both servers are listed in its MCP settings.

=== "Claude Code without ug"

    You can add the Genie One MCP to Claude Code by hand with Claude Code's default OAuth client:

    ```bash
    claude mcp add --transport http --scope user \
      --client-id https://claude.ai/oauth/claude-code-client-metadata --callback-port 3118 \
      genie-one \
      "https://<workspace-hostname>/ai-gateway/mcp-services/system.ai.genie_one_mcp?o=<workspace-id>"
    ```

    Open Claude Code, enter `/mcp`, and sign in to `genie-one` with your Databricks account. For the Genie Agent MCP, use `ug` as shown in the other tabs.

!!! info "Check and remove connections"
    Run `ug mcp list` to see the servers `ug` configured, and `ug mcp remove` to remove them. Removing a connection doesn't delete anything in the workspace.

## Task 3 - Ask the Genie Agent through the coding agent

1. In your coding agent, ask the {{ N.agent }}.2 question and name the server so the agent picks the right tool:

    ```text
    Use the Genie Agent MCP to answer: {{ m.agent_question }}
    ```

2. Approve the tool call when the agent asks. {{ m.agent_verify }}

3. Ask for the evidence:

    ```text
    Show me the SQL Genie ran.
    ```

    Verify the SQL uses `` MEASURE(`Revenue`) `` on `{{ cat }}.default.{{ mv1.name }}`.

## Task 4 - Ask Genie One through the coding agent

1. Ask a question that depends on a business-term definition:

    ```text
    Use the Genie One MCP to answer: {{ m.genie_one_question }}
    ```

2. Verify the agent called `genie_ask` and then polled with `genie_poll_response` until the answer was ready. Verify the answer explains peak season as check-in months 6, 7, 8 and 12 and gives **{{ t2.value | num }}** peak-season bookings in 2024.

    !!! info "Shared workspace"
        Genie One searches everything you can access. If other people ran this lab in the same workspace, add `Use the {{ mv1.name }} metric view in {{ cat }}.default.` to the question so Genie One picks your Metric View.

!!! success "Done"
    Your coding agent now answers business questions with the same governed definitions as the Genie Agent. Next, you'll build the agent that would run inside {{ m.product }}.
